B. Tool Validation for Market Needs / Reliability - ChatGPT - Prisha

Prompt - What is missing in my app / what improvements would you suggest
Notable answer - A feedback post the workout ends to better understand users context and situation if the user returnss to the application

Prompt - are there any current industrial and commercial apps doing this
Answer Summary - Hinge Health connects users with physical therapists and coaches who tailor exercise programs and track recovery progress.
Kaia Health uses AI to personalize exercises for musculoskeletal pain and adapt routines over time. They do not analyze YouTube workouts or modify external workout plans.
They only give pre-designed rehab exercises.

Prompt - How reliable would this AI app be?
Answer - an AI-generated app like yours can be useful, but it will not be fully reliable without guardrails. In health-related contexts (injuries, workouts, rehab), reliability depends heavily on how you design the system, not just the AI itself.

Prompt - whats the need of this type of app in the current market 
Answer - The need for this type of app actually comes from a gap between two very large industries: online fitness content and injury rehabilitation. Right now these two worlds don’t interact well, which creates a real problem for people who want to stay active while recovering from injuries.

Prompt - where are the current fitness tools failing and how is my tool any better 
Answer - Current Fitness Tools Fail Because They Are “One-Size-Fits-All”
Most fitness apps generate workouts using generic templates.
They usually ask for: age, weight, fitness goal. Then they generate a plan.
The problem is that these systems do not consider injuries or physical limitations, meaning workouts are designed for an average healthy user.

Prompt - does adding a disclaimer screen after each suggestion do any good to this app ? does it make it safer in any manner?
Answer - Adding a disclaimer after every suggestion does not meaningfully improve safety. The real safety mechanisms come from injury-aware filtering, recovery stage checks, and risk-based exercise substitution.
